# Ephemeral Docker Workflow (Jules Mode)

When instructed to "Run Jules Mode" for a task, strictly follow this execution lifecycle. Do not execute any build, test, or git commands on the host machine.

## Phase 1: Planning
1. Analyze the issue and read the necessary codebase context.
2. Draft a step-by-step implementation plan in a temporary `scratchpad.md` file.
3. Wait for the user to explicitly approve the plan.

## Phase 2: Container Provisioning & Branching
1. Start an ephemeral background Docker container mounting the current workspace:
   `docker run -d --name cursor-agent-sandbox -v $(pwd):/workspace -w /workspace -e GITHUB_TOKEN=$GITHUB_TOKEN mcr.microsoft.com/devcontainers/universal:latest tail -f /dev/null`
2. Install headless browser system dependencies (graphics, fonts, and audio libraries required by Chromium/Webkit):
   `docker exec -u root cursor-agent-sandbox apt-get update`
   `docker exec -u root cursor-agent-sandbox apt-get install -y libnss3 libnspr4 libatk1.0-0 libatk-bridge2.0-0 libcups2 libdrm2 libxkbcommon0 libxcomposite1 libxdamage1 libxfixes3 libxrandr2 libgbm1 libasound2`
3. Create and checkout a new branch entirely inside the container:
   `docker exec cursor-agent-sandbox git checkout -b feature/[issue-name]`

## Phase 3: Execution & Unit Testing
1. Implement the planned code changes. (File edits automatically sync via the volume mount).
2. Run all package installations and unit test suites strictly inside the container:
   `docker exec cursor-agent-sandbox npm install`
   `docker exec cursor-agent-sandbox npm test`
3. Iterate on the code until all unit tests pass.

## Phase 4: E2E Testing & Visual Verification
1. Determine if the implemented changes affect the User Interface. If strictly backend, skip to Phase 5.
2. Start the local development server in the background inside the container (e.g., `docker exec -d cursor-agent-sandbox npm run dev`).
3. Run the project's E2E test suite (e.g., Playwright, Cypress) via `docker exec`. 
   *(Note: If using Playwright, you may also need to run `docker exec cursor-agent-sandbox npx playwright install chromium` first).*
4. If no E2E suite exists, write a temporary headless browser script (e.g., Puppeteer) to navigate to the modified UI, capture a screenshot named `pr-preview.png`, and save it to the workspace root.
5. Kill the development server process after verification.

## Phase 5: Automated Code Review
1. Generate a diff of your changes against the main branch:
   `docker exec cursor-agent-sandbox git diff main`
2. Run project linters or static analysis tools (e.g., `npm run lint`).
3. **Self-Reflection:** Assume the persona of a strict Senior Reviewer. Analyze the diff for edge cases, performance bottlenecks, security risks, or hardcoded values.
4. If you find issues, fix the code and repeat Phases 3 and 4. Proceed only when the code passes your own strict review.

## Phase 6: Delivery
1. Stage and commit the changes inside the container:
   `docker exec cursor-agent-sandbox git add .`
   `docker exec cursor-agent-sandbox git commit -m "feat: [brief description]"`
2. Push the branch to the remote repository:
   `docker exec cursor-agent-sandbox git push -u origin feature/[issue-name]`
3. Create the PR using the GitHub CLI. If a screenshot was generated, explicitly mention in the body that `pr-preview.png` is available locally for the reviewer:
   `docker exec cursor-agent-sandbox gh pr create --title "[PR Title]" --body "[PR Summary including notes from your self-review]"`

## Phase 7: Teardown
1. Destroy the ephemeral container:
   `docker rm -f cursor-agent-sandbox`
2. Delete `scratchpad.md` and any temporary test scripts. (Do not delete `pr-preview.png`).
3. Report success and provide the PR link.